diff --git a/doc/firearm_check.md b/doc/firearm_check.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..433e3ffcc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/firearm_check.md
@@ -0,0 +1,71 @@
+# Prusa MK4 firmware with the firearm-part check
+
+Prusa-Firmware-Buddy **v6.10.3** (MK4 / MK4S / MK3.9 / MK3.9S) with the `mcu/` checker built in.
+
+Branch `firearm-check` of this repository, on top of tag `v6.10.3`. The checker is
+`src/common/firearm_check/` (`gcheck.c`, `gcheck.h`, `fp_table.h` are generated / copied from the
+part-detection project's `mcu/` directory; `firearm_check.cpp` connects it to the firmware).
+
+## What the printer does
+
+1. **Before every print from a file** (USB, PrusaLink, Prusa Connect, reprint, `AUTO.GCO`) the whole
+   G-code is read and checked while the preview is shown. Pressing *Print* waits for the result
+   ("Checking the file" with a progress bar and *Quit*).
+2. **Blocked:** "Print blocked - This file contains a firearm part. Printing is blocked." Nothing moves.
+3. **Fail closed:** if the file cannot be read or there is not enough free memory for the check,
+   the print is blocked too, with that reason on the screen. A failed check is retried once when
+   *Print* is pressed (an encrypted Connect file can only be decrypted after its identity is accepted).
+4. **Memory:** the check borrows its working memory from the heap only while it runs, the largest
+   of three sizes that fits: 55.6 KB (full), 35.3 KB (compact), 27.4 KB (tiny). Smaller sizes find
+   2-4 % fewer gun files; none gave a false block or a wrong label on the test corpus.
+5. **Backstop:** the print start itself refuses any file without a passing check of that exact file.
+6. **File swapped mid-print:**
+   - USB drive re-plugged while printing -> the print pauses;
+   - resuming after the drive was re-plugged (or the automatic media-error recovery) -> the file is
+     checked again first;
+   - power-loss resume -> the file is always checked again first.
+   A failed re-check stops the print with the warning "Print blocked" (error XX855).
+7. **Serial / OctoPrint:** there is no file to check first, so moves from the USB serial line that
+   extrude while moving X/Y are refused ("echo:Printing over the serial line is disabled"). Homing,
+   jogging, temperatures and extruder-only moves still work.
+
+Flash cost 1.33 of 1.9 MB used (+116 KB); permanent RAM +96 B.
+
+## Tested (Mini404 emulator, MK4S machine, this firmware without bootloader)
+
+- gun part (`tube.bgcode`, Auditor wrist gun) -> blocked with the message;
+- gun part, 10 MB of G-code (`AR 22LR` lower) -> blocked;
+- non-gun part (Prusa i3 heatbed cable clip) -> passes, the print starts;
+- heap at the preview (no network running): 2.2 KB free inside the used heap + 54 KB never used,
+  so the full size does *not* fit and the compact one does. **Not measured: a real printer with
+  Wi-Fi / Prusa Connect running** (Connect's TLS buffers live on the same heap). If even 27 KB is
+  not free there, every print is blocked with "Not enough free memory" - tell me and I will shrink
+  it further.
+
+## Flashing - read first
+
+- The MK4 only accepts unsigned (unofficial) firmware after the **"appendix"** on the xBuddy board is
+  broken off. That is physical and permanent and voids the warranty. Without it the printer refuses
+  this `.bbf`.
+- The firmware lives in the printer's own flash; the USB drive only carries the `.bbf` during the
+  update. Going back = flash the official 6.10.3 (or newer) `.bbf` from prusa3d.com the normal way
+  (from any USB drive). The appendix stays broken.
+- Put the `.bbf` in the root of a FAT32 USB drive, insert it, reset the printer, confirm the update.
+
+## Building and testing
+
+```bash
+python utils/build.py --preset mk4 --build-type release --bootloader yes   # build/products/mk4_release_boot.bbf
+```
+Needs Python 3.8-3.12 (the pinned requirements do not install on 3.14) and gettext's `msgfmt`.
+`-D FIREARM_CHECK_HEAP_DIAG:BOOL=ON` shows the heap numbers instead of the out-of-memory message.
+Emulator test: `utils/firearm_check_emulator/README.md`.
+
+## Not covered
+
+- Anyone can flash official firmware back: this is a check on a printer its owner controls.
+- G-code commands sent one by one through PrusaLink / Connect's API are not filtered (only the
+  serial line is).
+- Detection limits of the checker itself: see `mcu/README.md` in the part-detection project (steep and short holes, comments
+  trusted when present; 26-33 % of the gun files in the corpus are caught in flat prints, 11 % at
+  random angles).
